# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. github.com/Danfr4nk/wiki-brain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The message corpus is not Dan's data alone. It contains the phone numbers,
# email addresses and private words of ~499 other people, none of whom chose
# to be published. The earlier decision to track `intake/events.jsonl` in a
# public repo was Dan's to make about Dan. This is not the same decision and
# it is not his alone to make, so the default here is closed.
#
# git history cannot be un-published. Un-ignoring any line below puts that
# data on the public internet permanently, for everyone in it.
#
# To reverse: make the repository private FIRST, verify it, and only then
# decide whether these lines are still wanted. In that order.

# Raw corpus — every message, in the clear.
corpus/messages.csv

# Identity material and the hashing salt. The salt is what stops a published
# thread_id from being brute-forced back to a phone number: there are only
# ~10^10 US numbers, so an unsalted hash is not anonymisation, it is a speed
# bump. If this leaks, every derived artifact de-anonymises with it.
corpus/private/

# Shelved fragment exports — superseded, retained locally as error evidence.
# See CORPUS_POLICY.md. Same personal data as the corpus, same reasoning.
#
# Note what these lines do NOT do: the source Google Sheet is deliberately
# shared "anyone with the link" (see corpus/README.md), so the corpus is
# already reachable by URL. Ignoring it here keeps it out of git history,
# which is permanent and searchable. That is worth doing on its own terms —
# but it is not what is protecting this data, and it should not be mistaken
# for that.
shelf/**
!shelf/README.md
!shelf/MANIFEST.md
